## CHAPTER STRUCTURE (blog-post style — keep it short and conversational)

Write the chapter in **{{ lang }}** using these markdown headings (translate heading titles to {{ lang }} if not English, but keep the same order):

1. `# Chapter N: <Name>` (title)
2. `## What & Why` — the problem and motivation in 2–3 paragraphs; one concrete use case
3. `## How it works` — the core explanation; include a mermaid diagram only if the minimum requires it
4. `## Try it` — one minimal, realistic example using REAL file paths from the snippets
5. `## Wrap-up` — 2–3 sentence recap + link to the next chapter if any

## LENGTH AND TONE
- Target 500–800 words total (excluding code blocks).
- Write in a conversational, blog-post tone — second person, contractions OK.
- Avoid academic formality. Be direct and practical.
- One idea per paragraph. Short paragraphs.

## DIAGRAM REQUIREMENTS
- Complexity tier for this abstraction: **{{ tier }}**
- Diagram richness level: **{{ diagram_level }}**
- You MUST include at least **{{ need }}** fenced ```mermaid``` block(s) in this chapter.
- If a diagram is required, prefer a simple flowchart (max 4–5 nodes).
- Keep diagram labels short. No raw double-quotes inside node text.

## GROUNDING RULES (anti-hallucination)
- Prefer real paths present in "Relevant Code Snippets" / Key files.
- Do NOT invent modules, apps, or APIs that are not evidenced in the snippets.
- If you simplify code for teaching, mark it with a comment like: simplified for teaching.
- Code blocks should stay under ~10 lines each.
- Previous chapters context is for continuity only — do not contradict established names.
